{ To me the most iconic Dragon tactics }
12... Nxf3!! (12... Qxa2+ 13. Nxa2 { Nope ! }) (12... Nxe4 { Nice try buddy } 13. fxe4 $18) (12... Rfc8 { Too slow ! }) (12... Rac8 { Too slow ! }) (12... b5 13. Nd5! $18 { Black queen and e7 pawn are under attack }) 13. Nxf3 Nxe4! 14. Qd3 (14. Nxe4 Qxa2+ 15. Kc1 Bxb2#) 14... Nxc3+ 15. bxc3 Bxc3 $19 { There is no defense against Qb4, Qa2 or even Qa3 } *

[Event "Aosta op 12th"]
[Site "Saint Vincent"]
[Date "2004.02.09"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Manca, Federico"]
[Black "Cebalo, Miso"]
[Result "*"]
[WhiteElo "2363"]
[BlackElo "2475"]
[Annotator "alexa"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Opening mastery from level 1 to 10 (Dragon edition)"]
[ChapterName "Level 5 : thematic ideas 1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/9pyrf6Aq"]
[FEN "r1r3k1/pp1bppbp/3p1np1/q3n2P/3NP3/1BN1BP2/PPPQ2P1/2KR3R b - - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ Level 5 : thematic ideas
To know the moves is good, to know the tactics is good, but you also have to know the recurring ideas. Razor-blade tactical skills and deep understanding ! }
13... Rxc3 { Typical c3 exchange sacrifice, a very common idea in the sicilian, especially in the Dragon and the Najdorf.
This is very often seen as a way to launch an attack on the white king but this exchange sac is also a way to create long term static weaknesses in the queenside in order to exploit them during the endgame, like in the game. } 14. Qxc3 (14. bxc3 Nxh5 $13 { Black has now some pretentions over white king }) 14... Qxc3 15. bxc3 Nxh5 { An exchange sac usually comes with a little bonus, in order to get full compensation rather than speculation. } 16. g4 Nf6 { Note of Cebalo himself :
"Houdini gives White a miniscule advantage here. Of course this has no bearing on reality whatsoever and is simply a product of programming code, since here Black's compensation depends on factors which I believe are still somewhat beyond computers."
Black has no other weakness than being an exchange down. In compensation, he has a better structure, a h pawn (could be a passer one day) and many good minor pieces, while the rook on the C-file can put some pressure like we've seen previously. The game is very balanced overrall but like Carlsen once said, the computer evaluation does not indicate which side has the easier play. } *

