{ I lost that game and discovered during the analysis a fantastic idea which I never showed or played yet ! }
13... Nd7 (13... Nxd5 14. exd5 $16 { The E-file is a pain for Black here ! }) 14. Bg5 Bxd5 15. Bxd5 h6!! 16. Bxa8 { Another exchange sac ! } 16... hxg5 17. Bd5 Bxb2 { The little bonus, remember ? } 18. Rab1 Be5 { Black has a very strong grip on black squares. The following moves are highly instructive ! } 19. Qd2 (19. a4 Nc5 $19) 19... e6 20. Bb3 { Our Be5 is doing an incredible job. True dragon bishop ! } 20... Qf6 21. a4 { White must open somehow something for his rooks } 21... bxa4 22. Bxa4 Nc5 23. Bb3 Kg7 24. Qe3 Rh8 25. Red1 Rh4 { Black position is a wonder of harmony and coordination. It has something of a perfection lying in the subtle arrangement of the pieces. Not a single pawn or piece is unprotected, everybody is working together. } *

[Event "corr"]
[Date "1985.??.??"]
[White "David Koval"]
[Black "G Berman"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "alexandre"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B77"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Dragon Variation, Yugoslav Attack"]
[StudyName "Opening mastery from level 1 to 10 (Dragon edition)"]
[ChapterName "Level 6 : some model games 1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/f2frqIUQ"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ Level 6 : some model games
It's important to have some games in mind. You don't need to remember them move by move, but globally. It will help you during the game not to feel alone in the dark. In my own games, when my theory bag is empty, I always try to find my way thanks to the model games I know (plus my own reflexion of course). }
1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 Nc6 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Bc4 { Yougoslav attack } 9... Bd7 10. h4 Rc8 11. Bb3 h5 12. O-O-O Ne5 13. Bg5 { The dragon was fine and then Karpov found 13. Bg5 ! What is the purpose of that move ?
- Pressure on f6-e7-d8 in relation with some f4-e5 or Nd5
- After f4, Ng4 doesn't attack Be3 anymore
- Nc4 doesn't attack Be3 anymore
- Evacuate the E-file for some Rhe1 with some ideas in the center rather than on kingside
- Bh6 right now isn't convincing and after all, Bh6 is still playable
It took many years at that time (pre computer time) to realize the Yougo was not only a matter of speed but also a matter of well-built attack, hence Bg5 } (13. Bh6 { See chapter 10 : expert theory ! }) 13... Rc5 { Dragon players got back to their vault for quite a time, waited patiently in the dark,and then one day, Korchnoi came back to the city with 13...Rc5 ! Perfect answer against Bg5 :
- Prepare b5
- Free c8 for the Rf8
- Protect the potentiel Qa5 against the discovered attack Nc3-d5
- Allows some exchange sac on a future Nd5
The dragon was rehabilitated ! } 14. g4 hxg4 15. Bxf6? { It removes one of the best defender of black king, the Nf6 and it allows a quick and undisputed h5, but the price is very high... now the Dragon bishop will reign alone on his long diagonal ! } (15. h5 Nxh5 { Some might be scared to offer a second pawn hence 15. Bxf6 }) 15... Bxf6 16. h5 g5! { Not very good looking but let's not get killed right ? } 17. Nd5 Rxd5! { Thank you Viktor } (17... Bg7 18. Qxg5 e6 $13) 18. exd5 (18. Bxd5 e6 19. Bb3 gxf3) 18... gxf3 { As we have seen, and as we will see later on, in the Dragon, the material is not the Alpha and the Omega. We want two things : a good attack and a defense just good enough to deliver the holy fire first to our opponent.
Now the black king is safe for the few moves to come and we still have good prospects against white king. Some good endgame perspectives also but this is something we will cover extensively in level 8 ! } 19. c3 (19. Rdg1 g4 $19) 19... g4 (19... Qb6 { More precise, gaining some time on the counter attack. We will play g4 only if white plays something like Rg1 }) 20. Kb1 Qb6 21. Bc2 (21. Qh6 { Kept some chances }) 21... Nc4 22. Qc1 Rc8 23. Nf5 Na3+ 24. Ka1 Rxc3! 25. Nxe7+ (25. bxc3 Bxc3+ 26. Qb2 Bxb2# { Aesthetic rule #1 : never mate with a queen if another piece can deliver }) 25... Kf8 *

[Event "RUS-ch"]
[Site "Elista RUS"]
[Date "1995.10.??"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Sergey Daniliuk"]
[Black "Vladimir Malakhov"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "alexandre"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B74"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Dragon Variation, Classical Variation, Normal Line"]
[StudyName "Opening mastery from level 1 to 10 (Dragon edition)"]
[ChapterName "Level 6 : some model games 2"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/rjZ19wFM"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 g6 { Hyper accelerated Dragon } 3. d4 Bg7 4. Nc3 (4. dxc5 Qa5+ $13) 4... cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. Nb3 Nf6 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O d6 { This is the classical Dragon, where we don't have the opposite castling, but a more calm and positional game } 9. Be3 Be6 10. f4 Rc8 11. f5 Bd7 { It might looks strange considering we just play Be6 but we now have gained the e5 square, usually for the Nc6 } 12. g4 Ne5 { Pro tip : the pawnstorm g4-f5 is usually fully compensated by the powerful Ne5 } 13. Nd2 Rxc3 { The exchange sac is never far... } 14. bxc3 Bc6 15. Bf3 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 d5! { The queenside is destroyed, the kingside is wide open, so Black blast open the position ! } 17. Bd4 (17. e5 d4! 18. Qe2 dxe3 19. exf6 Bh6! 20. Nf3 exf6 $19) (17. exd5 Nxd5 $19 { Just look at the bishop pair ! }) 17... dxe4 18. Qh3 Nxg4! { Great tactical shot } 19. Bxg7 (19. Qxg4 Bxd4+ 20. cxd4 Qxd4+ 21. Kh1 (21. Rf2 Qxa1+ $19) (21. Kg2 Qxd2+ $19) 21... e3+ $19) 19... Kxg7 20. Nxe4 (20. Qxg4 Qxd2 $19) 20... Qb6+ 21. Nf2 gxf5 { White gives up ! Rg8 is coming and there is no safe room for the king. Great game from Malakhov featuring some interesting ideas against the classical line. } *

