[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/lOy05iEi"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 (5... a6) 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. O-O-O { The modern (not so modern now) line, allowing d5 } 9... d5 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Bh6 e6 (11... Qa5 { Much more active }) 12. h4 { Black is in big trouble, and as a Dragon player, you will have to deal with the backfire of an ambitious but risky opening. That's why you need to have some games of reference for such situations. } 12... Bh8 13. h5 (13. Bxf8 Qxf8 { We've already seen that standard idea }) 13... Nxh5 14. Bxf8? (14. g4 Ng3 15. Rh3 $18 { The critical line, unlike in the game, Kudrin has to take on f1 and accelerate White's attack. }) 14... Qxf8 15. g4 Ng3 16. Rh3 Rb8 { With some Qb4 idea } 17. a3 (17. Rxg3 Qb4! (17... Rxb2 18. Nb1! $13) 18. b3 Bxc3 $19) 17... Be5 18. Qf2 Bf4+! { Entrenching the bishop in the hole of White pawn structure } (18... Nxf1?? 19. f4 { And all of a sudden, allowing f4, Black has not that grip on black squares he used to have }) 19. Kb1 Nxf1 20. Rxf1 Qxa3 21. Nd1 Be5 22. c3 Ba6 (22... Bf4! { Then Ba6 threatening Bd3 and Bxf1 }) 23. f4 Bxf1 24. Qxf1 Bg7 25. e5 Qa4 26. Qf3 g5! { Free the Dragon bishop ! } 27. fxg5 Bxe5 28. Qd3 Qxg4 29. Qxh7+ Kf8 30. Ne3 Qe2 31. Nc2 Ke7 32. Re3 (32. g6 Qd1+ 33. Ka2 Qxc2 34. Qxf7+ Kd6 $19) 32... Qd1+ 33. Ka2 Rh8 { The endgame after Qd3 Qxd3 is dead lost and White gives up. } *

