{ Level 9 : typical endgame
When I look for an opening book, the first thing that I do is searching for a chapter about the endgame of the opening.

Beyond all the theoretical stuff (you will forget many of your variations in a few months anyway), what remains is your comprehension of the opening. To understand your opening, you have to study the pawn structure. There are many openings, and there are many pawn structures (I recommand you by the way the excellent book from Flores Rios : Chess structures).

Looking at some typical endgames arising from your opening is a good way not only to know the plan in the endgame (very much linked with the pawns in general) but also to improve your overall comprehension of the opening.

Example : let's pretend I know quite well my opening endgames. If I spot a good version of it (improved pawn structure for instance), I'll go for it as soon as possible ! }
 *

