{ The reader will recognize the beginning of the Yougoslav attack }
15... Nxh5 { Grab the pawn (1) } 16. g4 Nf6 17. Nb3 Qd8 18. e5!? Nxg4!! { Grab the pawn (2) } (18... dxe5?? 19. g5 $18 { Typical dragon tactics you gotta be aware of }) 19. fxg4 Bxg4 { Grab the pawn (3) } 20. Rdg1 dxe5! { Grab the pawn (4) } 21. Qxd8+ Rxd8 22. Na5 Rxc3! 23. bxc3 h5 { Very funny position. Beyond the fun, keep in mind this is a way (not the only one) to deal with the Yougo. Now we reach what I call the Yougoslave endgame. Not many pieces, but many pawns. } 24. Nb3 e4 { Who can sit at Geller's table and say "I have more passed pawns than you do" ? } 25. Nc5 Rd6!? (25... f5 26. Ne6 Rd6 27. Nxg7 Kxg7 $19) 26. Nxb7 (26. Nxe4 Re6 $19) 26... Rc6 27. Nc5 f5 28. Nb3 (28. Bd4 e5 $19 { Pawns, pawns everywhere ! }) 28... Rxc3 29. Bg5 Kf7 30. Kc1 Rc4 31. Kd2 Bc3+ 32. Ke3 e5 33. Rf1 a5 34. Nd2? Bd4# { Masterclass from Geller and highly instructive game and endgame } *

[Event "Kasparov - Anand World Championship Mat"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1995.09.28"]
[Round "11"]
[White "Viswanathan Anand"]
[Black "Garry Kasparov"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "alexandre"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Opening mastery from level 1 to 10 (Dragon edition)"]
[ChapterName "Level 9 : typical endgame 3"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/CvIf4CWz"]
[FEN "2r5/p2bppk1/3p2p1/1p1N3p/2r1P2P/5P2/PPPR2P1/1K5R b - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ An early trade queen brought a balanced endgame with an intact pawn structure !
Both sides have two pawn islands and a semi-open file for the rooks. But the C file is putting some pressure on the c2 pawn while the D file is not doing much. The very active black rooks will allow them to conduct a minority attack (b and c against abc). }
21... Kf8 (21... e6 22. Ne3 $16 { Would leave black with a horribly weak d6 pawn }) 22. Re1 Rb8 { Kasparov wants to play a5. } (22... a5?? 23. Nb6 $18) 23. b3 (23. e5 dxe5 24. Rxe5 Rxh4 $19) 23... Rc5 24. Nf4 (24. e5 dxe5 25. Rxe5 Be6 $15 { Unpleasant pin }) 24... Rbc8 { Since black is not threatening Nb6 anymore, Kasparov prefer to double the rooks along the C-file } 25. Kb2 a5 { Minority attack ! Of course, Kasparov is not going for the mate, he just tries to increase the activity and the pressure and maybe white will blunder ! } 26. a3 Kg7 { Sometimes, we can the see the black king doing some infiltration in the white territory via f6-e5-f4-g3, attacking h4, especially if white has a white square bishop instead of a knight. Remember that ! } 27. Nd5 Be6 28. b4? (28. Nxe7! Re8 29. Nd5 Bxd5 { e pawn is pined but } 30. b4! axb4 31. axb4 Rc4 32. Rxd5 Rxb4+ 33. Kc3 Rc4+ 34. Kb3 f5 35. Rxb5 Rec8 $13 { With some balanced rooks ending. }) 28... axb4 29. axb4 Rc4 30. Nb6?? { And Anand felt in Kasparov trap ! } (30. c3 Bxd5 31. Rxd5 Rxc3 32. Re2 Rc1 33. Rxb5 Rh1 { Complicated endgame }) 30... Rxb4+ 31. Ka3 { Both rooks are under attack and unable to mutually protect itselves } 31... Rxc2!! { Interesting endgame where we have to remember the pressure along the C-file, the minority attack, and that idea of a king walk from g7 to e5 } *

