[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/JJQAfrrl/KF35AbyF"]
[FEN "3q1rk1/p2bppb1/3p3B/1p1B3p/4P3/5p2/PPPQ4/1K1R4 b - - 0 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ Level 10 : some expert theory
Not to be offensive, but you shouldn't even consider this level if all the previous points are not validated, which means you'll be way above 2000 F elo.

Important point : not all the openings require the same exact level of theory / understanding. There are many systems which rely more on comprehension, good appreciation, and some openings, especially the sharpest one, where you have to be well prepared.

Also : it always depends on the strength of the adversity ! Don't waste your time on theory if you only play on internet, because internet chess is not very theory-friendly. If you're a lichess or chess.com addict, you rather should focus on the dangerous side lines people are very fond of (you know, all the CATCHY YOUTUBE VIDEOS ABOUT OPENING TRAPS) }
21... e5! (21... e6? 22. Rg1 $13) (21... Bg4?? 22. Qg5 $19) 22. Bg5 (22. Rg1 Bg4 $19) 22... Bf6 23. Bh6 Bg4! { It was an improvement I found in Gawain Jones book on dragon and has not been played in the lichess masters database, but someone probably played it somewhere in the world before I did ! } (23... Bg7 24. Bg5 Bf6 25. Bh6 Bg7 26. Bg5 Bf6 { Wan, Yunguo - Emiroglu, Cankut, 1/2-1/2, Albena Masters op, 2012, https://lichess.org/NkWWljOi }) 24. Bxf8 { My theory ended up there and it gave me a 70 minutes advantage on my opponent (16 minutes vs 1h26 !) } 24... Qxf8 25. Bc6 Kg7 { I went on with an interesting plan I knew from the typical yougoslave dragon endgames (level 8) : pawns can finish the job ! } 26. Bxb5 (26. Qxd6 Qxd6 27. Rxd6 Kg6 28. Bxb5 h4 29. Bf1 h3 $19 { What is an exchange down worth for against two passed pawns on the 6th rank ? }) 26... Kg6 { Chess is often quite intuitive. Here I felt I needed my little army to march forward as a group. } 27. Qf2 Qe7 { I must not hurry and I have to find a way to trade the queens. Reducing the dynamism of the position can only make my endgame easier and stronger } 28. Rg1 Bg5 { Preventing Qxf3 } 29. Bd3 Qc7 { Slowly coming to the light } 30. Rg3 Qc5 31. Rxf3 Qxf2! { The right move, and the 4th exchange sac of the game ! I only need to promote one pawn } (31... Bxf3 32. Qxf3 { Probably winning for black but with Queens and opposite color bishops on the board, I figured white kept some practical chances }) 32. Rxf2 h4 33. Bc4 f6 { Not taking any risk whatsoever } 34. Bf1 Kh5 35. Bg2 h3 36. Bh1 Kh4 37. c3 Kg3 38. Rf1 Be2 39. Rg1+ Kf2 { The rook is trapped, the h pawn is too strong and the black king made his way from g8 to f2 to seal the deal ! } { 0-1 Black wins. } *

